il and prison and  immigration detention centers are  um super overcrowded very frequently  um the sanitation is is really a  challenge in these systems  there's poor ventilation there's sort of  dilapidated conditions  um people who are incarcerated are often  required to  provide their own to purchase their own  hygiene materials  at you know on the sort of inhumane  wages that they're they're given for  working in in these person systems so  um it's it's really a volcano ready to  explode  um so back in march and april activists  were already sounding the alarm around  this and we were lucky enough to work  with um three academics uh eric lofgren  christian loom  uh and nina peppermint along with my  colleague aaron horowitz at the aclu  to build an epidemiological model  of uh what would happen to the spread in  these systems  so first i want to talk about modeling  that one outbreak um  a little primer for folks who aren't  familiar um  with epidemiological models although  many more of us  are than used to be in the past  unfortunately because of what's going on  right now essentially in a compartmental  epidemiological model you move from  being  susceptible to a particular infection to  being exposed  to being infectious and able to spread  it to others and then  uh if you do not die from that infection  you are  recovered and so we can use a system of  differential equations to  um model sort of the transmission of a  disease through a population  this is what that looks like in math if  folks are are sort of mappy  this can be a little bit intimidating  but basically it's just saying that the  rate of transition between these two  these states is a function of some of  the natural disease parameters  so um with covid there are there are  sort of  average ranges that you spend in the uh  pre-infectious period the e compartment  the exposed compartment  and the infectious compartment for  example and it's also a function of  um the con effect of contact between  individuals who are infectious and who  are susceptible so  that itself is a function of both  natural history  parameters of the disease but also  things like how often  people are coming into effective contact  so this is what a really really basic  compartmental model looks like  in r we use the desolve package and  it's essentially just putting this up  here into code um  it's pretty straightforward this is a  really simplistic version of the model  and i don't want to spend too much time  talking  um about this i want to talk about the  challenges of scaling that up  um but uh this is  so so we're going through pictures math  and  code um this is the picture of what our  model looks like so not only are you  moving through  sei and r compartments but you can also  move from the community  through to booking and processing um  through the jail system  and and we parameterized this using real  values from  allegheny county the jail system which  published really um  really complete thorough sort of  estimates of how many people are being  arrested every day how many of those  people are being booked into jail  how many people in jail have a trial on  any given day  etc um just briefly that's what that  looks like in  math in our paper it's very complicated  but it's not very complex it's  straightforward as long as you can get  all of the parameters right  and again this is what this looks like  in code um  it's a lot but um it's again  straightforward once you can get  um and which is to say once you can  wrangle a bunch of academics to do all  the hard stuff for you  um this is what the baseline looks like  if we if we do nothing  the disease spreads through  the jail at a given at a much higher  rate than than through the community and  that's because  people in jails are coming into  effective contact at much higher  frequency than people who are  just in the in a community  um so essentially the the first step of  this was taking  um parameterizing that model to one  particular system  um running all of our code uh that was  the focus of the paper  and then we wanted to see sort of what  is the national scale  of um what is the impact at a national  level  if this outbreak happens in every jail  um and so we looked at every single jail  with a jail population of over  um 100 people uh which accounts for  around  90 of the population of the us um live  in a county with one of these jails  um and used a lot of open data that that  is made available to to parameterize  these outbreaks so i'm going to talk  about four challenges  of scaling up a single model in one  community to  essentially the nation so challenge one  we had all this great information from  allegheny county about the rates of  movement from jail to court the rates of  um  how frequently people are arrested the  average length of detention  um the size of the incarcerated  population the size of the jail staff  um who are are moving through these  systems um but we wanted to go from from  that one place to  um localizing this in various counties  and so that involves  um some parameters that are that are  natural disease history parameters of  cova-19  um and some sort of custom uh  pieces of information from from  different places um and this  was only possible because of open data  sets  that we were able to use from largely  from the vera institute  we used again a lot of information  specific to allegheny county we used  some census of jails data from the  bureau of justice statistics  um we used new york times data to  understand you know how far along  in the communities were each of these  infections at the time that we were  modeling them  and so that was  only this research was only possible  because of open public data we talked  earlier about  um uh about the importance of  of open data and also open science in  this process so that was  a huge one for us the second challenge  was  parallelizing this model so um  straightforward enough to 