l the  compartments and this is not  because people are dying from the  disease because those people remain in  the compartments  this is just people disappearing uh  because one of our terms wasn't paired  so  we were able to test visually when we're  modeling just one uh  outbreak but that's not really feasible  when we have 20  000 models um and so we were lucky  enough at the aclu to be able to bring  in our data or  data engineering team um i think  that's my recommendation strongly but  that's not available to everyone and so  um you can definitely bring in data  engineering best practices  one of which is is testing and making  sure that um  you're building in your assumptions and  failing pretty loudly when  your code is not meeting your  assumptions  um challenge number four that i wanted  to talk about that i think has been  mentioned in  in this kind of setting frequently um is  that when you're scaling up often we  didn't have data for  specific localities so one thing that we  um  one issue that we ran into when we were  trying to  um to scale up and and seeing some weird  outputs is that we had forgotten to set  our seed  um and we're getting different versions  across multiple different  um outputs and so this is again let me  join the chorus of folks to remind you  to set your seed  um especially if you're in this is a  really simple imputation to  um estimate the the size of the staff  population in  jail systems where we didn't know the  size of the staff population  um so i want to talk about some of the  scenarios that we ran and some of the  results and i'm moving really quickly  because this is  a ton of um stuff but i'll i'll make  these slides  available um and our paper is also  available publicly as well so  um we wanted to look at three main  interventions  um to potentially reduce the impact of  kova 19  in jails um we looked at reducing intake  um  so reducing arrests changing some  arrests to  um citations for example and just  reducing the number of the number of  arrests that are happening in the  community  um we looked at reducing arrests and  increasing the release  the speed of release for individuals  particularly in this case for medically  vulnerable individuals  and the aclu has taken a bunch of  lawsuits arguing that essentially  detaining people who are  are medically vulnerable in these  situations is a violation of their human  rights it puts them at too great over  risk  and finally we looked at sort of a  blanket um  collection of actions that we could take  within the jails for the people who do  remain  um as much as we would like it it's not  feasible right now  uh for us to be living in a jail-free  world  um and so so what can we do to  actually protect the people who are who  are still um behind bars  uh and essentially what we found is the  best way to  reduce transmission and to reduce  infections hospitalizations and deaths  in both  the jail and in the community is to  reduce arrest and bookings as much as  possible don't bring into people into  the system  because when you bring people into jail  systems in particular which have very  low rates  are very low average lengths of  detention um and in which people are  often cycling back  into the community you're not only  infecting those people  and and harming the people who are  incarcerated but you're also  um seeding that infection in the broader  community and really exaggerating the  problem so that was  that was the biggest thing that we could  do um we also found that  increasing the speed of releasing people  especially if you can act quickly and  early in the outbreak  um before it spreads throughout the jail  ideally um  then that really reduces cases and  deaths uh in the jails in the  communities especially  for medically vulnerable people so i  know these graphs are hard to read when  we're moving so quickly but um  in this middle row we're looking at  infections hospitalizations and deaths  of incarcerated people and and we see  um a real big jump in  decrease in deaths when we release  medically  vulnerable people from jail as quickly  as we can  and finally uh it's definitely necessary  to to do the  the collection of things that we know  are um  we're supposed to do to prevent the  spread of coca-19 in the community we  have to be able to make those available  in jails as well we have to give people  masks we have to uh improve sanitation  and hygiene we have to  um reduce the population population so  that socially distancing is even  possible  um and that's necessary but it's not  enough to stop transit  transmission um  so i want to talk about sort of like  when we run these 1200 models and  aggregate that together  and make some pretty uh sort of like  reasonable assumptions  about death rate about hospitalization  rates  based on on where we were in march and  the information that was  available at the time um we find that  across the board reducing arrests saves  lives it saves  tons of lives in the jails but it also  saves  hundreds of or tens of thousands of  lives in the community um  this isn't just an intervention it's  it's a very important intervention for  people who are incarcerated but it's not  just for them it's for  the for everybody and this is again an  example of when we  do the right thing by people who are  just involved and  involved in the criminal legal system uh  it it helps all of us  and this finding has been sort of  corroborated by two  additional studies one in chicago and  one in milwaukee  that essentially find that jail  community cycling is a big predictor of  cases of coronavirus  um in chicago they found that this was a  bigger predictor than  race a bigger predictor than poverty  than many of the other  risk factors that we know contribute to  covert 19 spread  that bringing people into the jail  system often um  not releasing them only because they  can't pay bail um  is is a huge risk factor uh  people maybe not working in this space  may not know that the majority of people  who are in jail  uh are detained pre-trial they haven't  been convicted yet  um and a majority of those people are  only in jail because they can't pay  can't afford their bail or bond right um  and so  the system doesn't have to be that way  we don't have to use cash bail  um these are these are interventions  that we can take right now  and this is important because uh for for  many reasons one because people's lives  are at risk  um but it also exacerbates the racial  disparities present in coven 19 cases  and deaths  so nationwide people black people are  dying at 2.5 times the rate of white  people  and it's even greater i think when you  look at age stratified rates  and that's for many reasons but a lot of  those get back to the systemic  disparities  so uh the graph that you're seeing on  the left is showing that  um black people are a disproportionate  number of the stops and searches  in um in washington dc and  we see that uh not only stops uh are  disproportionately of of black people  but that stops that don't lead to  anything so essentially useless stops  right um  stops that that probably are um  ones that that white people wouldn't be  stopped for um  are are even more disparate right and  and a lot of  um evidence is sort of corroborates this  like we know that there are racial  disparities at every  um every sort of phase of the system  and so in the minute that i have left i  want to talk about um  where how this is used um as of mid-july  of this year we'd filed over 161 legal  actions  related to covet 1969 in jails and  prisons  32 of these are class actions 51 in  immigration detention  and we've also used this in successful  advocacy with  our partners to release over 48 000  people from  from detention whether that's jail  prison or immigration detention so  really the strength of this work is only  useful if our advocates can take it and  run with it and these are the people  doing the real  work on the ground frankly um but i  wanted to give this this one example of  how  statistics and data science can be used  to um to sort of  support uh advocacy and and litigation  so um thanks very much sorry for my  technical difficulties um  the report is available on the aclu  website  um these slides are up on my github and  then i'm  on twitter if you want to talk about it  so thanks very much  you

