his space  may not know that the majority of people  who are in jail  uh are detained pre-trial they haven't  been convicted yet  um and a majority of those people are  only in jail because they can't pay  can't afford their bail or bond right um  and so  the system doesn't have to be that way  we don't have to use cash bail  um these are these are interventions  that we can take right now  and this is important because uh for for  many reasons one because people's lives  are at risk  um but it also exacerbates the racial  disparities present in coven 19 cases  and deaths  so nationwide people black people are  dying at 2.5 times the rate of white  people  and it's even greater i think when you  look at age stratified rates  and that's for many reasons but a lot of  those get back to the systemic  disparities  so uh the graph that you're seeing on  the left is showing that  um black people are a disproportionate  number of the stops and searches  in um in washington dc and  we see that uh not only stops uh are  disproportionately of of black people  but that stops that don't lead to  anything so essentially useless stops  right um  stops that that probably are um  ones that that white people wouldn't be  stopped for um  are are even more disparate right and  and a lot of  um evidence is sort of corroborates this  like we know that there are racial  disparities at every  um every sort of phase of the system  and so in the minute that i have left i  want to talk about um  where how this is used um as of mid-july  of this year we'd filed over 161 legal  actions  related to covet 1969 in jails and  prisons  32 of these are class actions 51 in  immigration detention  and we've also used this in successful  advocacy with  our partners to release over 48 000  people from  from detention whether that's jail  prison or immigration detention so  really the strength of this work is only  useful if our advocates can take it and  run with it and these are the people  doing the real  work on the ground frankly um but i  wanted to give this this one example of  how  statistics and data science can be used  to um to sort of  support uh advocacy and and litigation  so um thanks very much sorry for my  technical difficulties um  the report is available on the aclu  website  um these slides are up on my github and  then i'm  on twitter if you want to talk about it  so thanks very much  you
 

